==Post-match==
Arsenal players collapsed to the pitch in devastation upon the final whistle as they suffered extension to the trophy drought that began in 2005 when they beat [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in the [[2005 FA Cup Final|final]] of the [[2004–05 FA Cup]]. Manager [[Arsène Wenger]] admitted that the defeat was "very hard to take" but refused to blame anyone for it. He said: "The players are disappointed but we have to pick ourselves up, that's what a team is about. [...] We got what we expected from Birmingham. They are a team who fight very hard and cause a lot of problems from long balls. Congratulations to them, they got the trophy, but we have regrets with the way we conceded the [Martins] goal".<ref>{{Cite web | title=BBC Sport - Football - Carling Cup final: Wenger backs 'destroyed' Arsenal duo | url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/a/arsenal/9409056.stm | access-date=2026-09-05 | website=news.bbc.co.uk}}</ref> Despite the intention to recover from the defeat Arsenal would go on to suffer a significant drop in form, losing a great number of the remaining matches, being knocked out of the [[2010-11 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] and the [[2010-11 FA Cup|FA Cup]] and eventually finishing 4th place in the league.<ref>{{Cite web | title=History: Barcelona 3-1 Arsenal {{!}} UEFA Champions League 2010/11 {{!}} UEFA.com | url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2003764/ | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250222220809/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2003764/ | access-date=2026-09-05 | archive-date=2025-02-22}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | title=BBC Sport - Football - Manchester United 2-0 Arsenal | url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/9421189.stm | access-date=2026-09-05 | website=news.bbc.co.uk}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | title=BBC Sport - Football - Saturday football as it happened | url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/9483110.stm | access-date=2026-09-05 | website=news.bbc.co.uk}}</ref> They would not win a trophy until 2014, beating [[Hull City A.F.C.|Hull City]] in the [[2014 FA Cup Final|final]] of the [[2013–14 FA Cup]].<ref>{{Cite web | title=Arsenal 3-2 Hull City - BBC Sport | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27354148 | access-date=2026-09-05 | website=www.bbc.co.uk}}</ref>

Birmingham boss [[Alex McLeish]] hailed his team's triumph, saying that the final win was his "greatest achievement" as a manager, adding: "I am so pleased for the fans, they have craved victory for a long time. [...] For a small club like ours to beat Arsenal was a titanic effort. [...] No-one gave us a prayer, we were underdogs but sometimes the bookies don't get it right". Winning goalscorer [[Obafemi Martins]] commented that "it means a lot to the fans and the players because Birmingham really needed this trophy", while man-of-the-match [[Ben Foster (footballer)|Ben Foster]] reflected on the win saying "the main thing was that the lads kept fighting. You could see how tired we were but we just kept working and working. That's the spirit we've got".<ref>{{Cite web | title=BBC Sport - Football - Carling Cup final: McLeish hails 'greatest achievement' | url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/b/birmingham_city/9407612.stm | access-date=2026-09-05 | website=news.bbc.co.uk}}</ref> Birmingham would later go on to suffer relegation in the league during the season and were knocked out in the group stage of the [[2011–12 UEFA Europa League]] the following season.<ref>{{Cite web | title=Tottenham Hotspur 2-1 Birmingham City - BBC Sport | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13444710 | access-date=2026-09-05 | website=www.bbc.co.uk}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | title=Birmingham 1-0 NK Maribor - BBC Sport | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16081822 | access-date=2026-09-05 | website=www.bbc.co.uk}}</ref>

